# Stage0 digests, COMMITTED deliberately — see docs/release/bootstrap.md §1.
# Pinned stage0 version: 0.1.4. Changing stage0 means changing this file,
# which means a reviewable commit rather than a server-side change.
#
# Moved 0.1.3 -> 0.1.4 in #1857. The bump is load-bearing, not hygiene: 0.1.3's
# `parseFloat` has NO HEX-FLOAT BRANCH, so it lowers every `0x…p…` literal to
# ±0.0. `runtime/root` is the only place in the repo using such literals, and
# stage0 is what compiles `runtime/` into `libbitrt.a` — so a 0.1.3 stage0
# silently zeroed `bit_rt_log`'s entire polynomial and `log(x)` returned 0 for
# every input. 0.1.4 carries the fix.
#
# That is also why 0.1.4 could NOT be built by its predecessor, which
# dist/release.sh otherwise requires ("a release must be produced by the previous
# release, so a compiler bug introduced here cannot silently compile itself into
# the artifact"). The runtime's correctness depended on a fix that existed only
# downstream of the broken compiler, so the cycle was broken with the documented
# two-pass bootstrap via `BIT_STAGE0_BIN` (scripts/stage0.sh). That hatch exists
# for exactly this, and with 0.1.4 pinned it goes back to being unused.
#
# Verified before pinning, on aarch64-macOS:
#   - all three artifacts downloaded ANONYMOUSLY (no token) and matched these
#     digests. An authenticated fetch proves nothing: a draft release answers a
#     token-bearing request and 404s an anonymous one.
#   - the SHIPPED macos artifact computes log(e)=1, log(1000)=6.907755278982137,
#     log2(8)=3, log10(1000)=3. Under 0.1.3 every one of those was 0, so this is
#     the check that actually distinguishes the two — the release smoke test only
#     prints a string and would have passed either way.
#   - x86_64-linux smoke-tested on REAL hardware, not emulation
#   - tag v0.1.4 lands on 95f0c7d, the commit that was tested (#1856: `gh release
#     create` tags the REMOTE head, so main is pushed BEFORE the release is cut)
#
# Compare bootstrap stages built to the SAME basename in DIFFERENT directories.
# A Mach-O ad-hoc signature embeds the binary's own file name, so `-o /tmp/stage2`
# vs `-o /tmp/stage3` differ at one byte range for that reason alone and look
# like a broken fixed point.
#
# EXACTLY ONE LINE PER TRIPLE. dist/stage0-verify.sh matches on the triple rather
# than the full filename (the version moves, the triple does not) and refuses
# outright when a triple has two digests. So the previous release's digests are
# REPLACED here, not appended — §3's diverse-double-compiling check gets the
# N-1 artifact from the releases page, not from this file.
